# DESCRIPTION

The net service is the network stack, Plan 9's ip device as a service: IPv4 and IPv6 over one Ethernet device, with TCP and UDP above them, served as files.
Its manifest runs `net /net/ether0`, the first device devmgr(8) binds (ether(3)), and mounts devmgr's `net` at `/net` for it; the service serves its files at `net` in its tree, which a manifest mounts after the devices: `mount /net net/net after`.
dial(2) is how a program uses them.

`net` takes `-a` <address>`/`<prefix> and `-g` <gateway> before the device to configure IPv4.
Without them it asks for an address by DHCP (RFC 2131), probing the address it is offered by ARP before taking it, and renews the lease as it runs out; it says `net: 10.0.2.15/24 by DHCP` on the console when it has one.
IPv6 starts on the link-local address the device's address gives, checked for duplicates, then takes routers' adverts and an address in each prefix they advertise.
The stack answers ARP, neighbour solicitations and pings, sends with a TTL of 64, and drops fragments: it neither sends nor reassembles them.

All addresses are written as Plan 9 writes them: IPv4 dotted, IPv6 as RFC 5952 has it, `::` for none, and an address and port as <address>`!`<port>.

## Conversations

`tcp` and `udp` each have a `clone` and up to 32 conversations, numbered from 0, each a directory made the first time it is used.
Opening `clone` takes the first conversation not in use and gives a channel to its `ctl`; reading that channel gives the conversation's number.
A conversation stays in use while its `ctl`, `data` or `listen` is open, and when the last closes it is ended: a TCP connection closed gracefully, or reset if data it received was left unread; a listener closed, and the calls to it not yet taken reset; a UDP endpoint closed.

`ctl` takes these lines:

: `connect` <address>`!`<port> [<local>]
  Calls <address>.
  For TCP the write waits until the handshake has ended, and fails if it did not succeed; `err` then says why: `connection refused`, `connection timed out`.
  The local port is the stack's choice, from 49152 up; UDP takes <local> if given.
: `announce` [<address>`!`]<port>
  Takes calls to <port> on any address of ours; <address>, `*` or one of ours, is accepted and not otherwise used.
  Port 0 or `*` is a free one, from 49152 up.
: `hangup`
  TCP: ends the connection with a reset.
: `close`
  TCP: sends what was written, then the end of the stream; reads go on until the peer's end.
: `headers`
  UDP: each datagram read and written begins with a 52-byte record, as Plan 9's `Udphdr`: the remote address (16 bytes), the local address (16), the interface's address (16), the remote port and the local port (2 each, big-endian).
  A datagram is written to the record's remote address and port.
: `keepalive`, `ttl`, `tos`, `ignoreadvice`, `bind`
  Accepted, and do nothing.

Any other line is refused.

`data` is the conversation's bytes.
On TCP a read returns what has arrived, and waits until something has; it returns nothing at the stream's end.
A write takes what the connection's 64 KiB send buffer has room for and waits while it has none; a write after the connection has ended fails.
On UDP each read is one datagram, and on a connected conversation only those from the address and port it connected to; each write is one datagram.
Both can be read and written through the one ring (node(5)).

A program waits for a call by opening `listen` in an announced TCP conversation and reading it: the read waits for a call and returns its conversation's number.
The channel holds that new conversation as its ctl would, until it is closed.

`local` and `remote` read as <address>`!`<port>.
`status` reads as the state, Plan 9's name for it (`Closed`, `Listen`, `Syn_sent`, `Established`, `Close_wait`, ...), then `qin` <n>, the bytes or datagrams waiting to be read, and `qout` <n>, the bytes not yet acknowledged.
UDP's state is `Open` or `Closed`.
`err` is why the conversation last failed.
A protocol's `stats` counts its segments or datagrams, a name and a number a line.

## The interface

`ipifc/0` is the one interface, on the device.
Its `status` is Plan 9's: a line beginning `device /net/ether0 maxtu 1514`, with `pktin` and `pktout` counting frames, then an address a line, each its address, mask, network, and valid and preferred lifetimes in milliseconds, a lease's or `4294967295` for none.
Its `ctl` takes `add` <address> <mask> (or `try`), which gives the interface that IPv4 address, and `remove` <address> <mask>, which takes it away.
A mask is dotted, `255.255.255.0`, or a prefix, `/24`, and IPv4's prefixes print as IPv6's, so a /24 is `/120`.
`local` lists its addresses.

`iproute` reads as one route a line: target, mask, next hop, type (`4`, `4i` on the link, `4u` our address, `6...` the same for IPv6), tag, interface, and source and its mask.
The routes follow from the interface's addresses, the gateway and IPv6's routers; writing `add 0 0` <gateway> sets IPv4's default route and `del 0 0` removes it.

`arp` reads as the neighbour cache, a line each: `ether`, `OK` or `WAIT`, the address, the device address in hex once known, and our address it was found from.
`ipselftab` lists the addresses taken as ours, with their types.
`ndb` is what DHCP gave, as ipconfig writes it for cs and dns: `ip=`, `ipmask=` and `ipgw=` on a line, then a `dns=` line for each name server; empty until there is an address.

# EXAMPLES

A session's addresses:

```rc
% cat /net/ndb
ip=10.0.2.15 ipmask=255.255.255.0 ipgw=10.0.2.2
	dns=10.0.2.3
```

